# ── Chapter 14 fidelity pins: "When the System Notices It Broke" ─────────────
#
# KIND: CONTENT pin. Run:
#   nibli-pin --kb new-book-plans/constitution.nibli \
#             book-1/14-when-the-system-notices-it-broke.pins.nibli
#
# "Nothing reads the duty" is an absence and cannot be queried. It used to sit
# here as a grep in a comment that nobody ran; since nibli 80a87e9 it is a real
# precondition, with its own positive control so an empty result cannot mean the
# command broke. Needs `--allow-shell`, which verify.sh passes.
#
# DEFECT PINS — half this file encodes defects rather than guarantees, and the
# repairs are tracked. `err(·, Isolation)` on the seven prisoners flips FALSE the
# moment the isolation marker is repaired or any `meets` fact is ever recorded,
# and `err(Ruk/Lalo, Placement)` flips FALSE when the placement marker is keyed
# on where someone was PUT rather than on having a home. Either repair makes the
# chapter's middle section wrong, which is the intended outcome — a flip here is
# the fix arriving, not a regression.

:expect-pins 31

# The chain must END: `err` feeds `obliged` (Article 8b) and nothing reads the
# duty. If this fires, the design grew a third link and the chapter's closing
# argument has to be rewritten around it.
:require test -z "$(awk -F'->' '/^[^#]/ && /->/ && $1 ~ /obliged/ {print}' new-book-plans/constitution.nibli)"

# POSITIVE CONTROL — the same command against a relation that IS read must find
# lines, so an empty result above means "unread" and not "the awk is broken".
:require test -n "$(awk -F'->' '/^[^#]/ && /->/ && $1 ~ /false/ {print}' new-book-plans/constitution.nibli)"

# "Adam, Hano, Lalo, Nando, Don, Kel, Ruk — the seven, without exception."
# ALL SEVEN are pinned deliberately. An earlier draft named four and said "every
# convicted person in the society, without exception"; Adam, Kel and Lalo were
# missing and the marker fires on all three. A prose enumeration is only as good
# as the pins under it, so the enumeration is pinned entry by entry.
:defect "repairing the isolation marker, or any `meets` fact ever being recorded"
? err(Adam, Isolation).
# => TRUE

:defect "repairing the isolation marker, or any `meets` fact ever being recorded"
? err(Hano, Isolation).
# => TRUE

:defect "repairing the isolation marker, or any `meets` fact ever being recorded"
? err(Lalo, Isolation).
# => TRUE

:defect "repairing the isolation marker, or any `meets` fact ever being recorded"
? err(Nando, Isolation).
# => TRUE

:defect "repairing the isolation marker, or any `meets` fact ever being recorded"
? err(Don, Isolation).
# => TRUE

:defect "repairing the isolation marker, or any `meets` fact ever being recorded"
? err(Kel, Isolation).
# => TRUE

:defect "repairing the isolation marker, or any `meets` fact ever being recorded"
? err(Ruk, Isolation).
# => TRUE

# "Several of the judged are not held: one was cleared on appeal, and the others
#  are protected for having exposed an authority." Nia is the clearance; Rebel,
#  Sly and (since v0.7) Rex are the shields — so the marker is silent on them
#  despite a judgment. The prose used to count these and the count went stale the
#  moment a cast member was added; it now states the rule, and the roster is
#  pinned name by name instead, Rex included below.
? err(Nia, Isolation).
# => FALSE

? err(Rebel, Isolation).
# => FALSE

? err(Sly, Isolation).
# => FALSE

# "Ask which free people are, and the answer is none."
? err(Bela, Isolation).
# => FALSE

? err(Jala, Isolation).
# => FALSE

# "nobody anywhere in this society has company on record"
? meets(Hano).
# => FALSE

? meets(Bela).
# => FALSE

# the placement marker: right about nobody
:defect "keying err/2 on where somebody was PUT, not on having a home"
? err(Ruk, Placement).
# => TRUE

:defect "keying err/2 on where somebody was PUT, not on having a home"
? err(Lalo, Placement).
# => TRUE

? err(Hano, Placement).
# => FALSE

# and Ruk is correctly placed, which is what makes the marker wrong
? building(HighSec, Ruk).
# => TRUE

# ── v0.8: the audit produces a duty (Article 8b) ─────────────────────────────
# "Where the placement marker fires, the review body has a duty about that
#  person. Where the isolation marker fires, the same."
? obliged(Review, Ruk).
# => TRUE

? obliged(Review, Lalo).
# => TRUE

# via the isolation marker, which fires on every prisoner — so the duty is owed
# about all of them too. "a duty owed to a category rather than to a case."
? obliged(Review, Adam).
# => TRUE

? obliged(Review, Hano).
# => TRUE

# THE ARGUMENT-ORDER DISCRIMINATOR — DO NOT DROP EITHER HALF. Writing the duty
# backwards leaves the four pins above TRUE while silently meaning the PRISONER is
# bound to the Review. These two are the only thing that tells the versions apart.
# [2026-07-31] The typo route closed upstream — `obligated` was renamed
# `obligated_by`, so that spelling no longer compiles. The converse mechanism
# survives under the new name, and a slip within `obliged` itself was never
# covered by anything but these two lines.
? obliged(Ruk, Review).
# => FALSE

? obliged(Adam, Review).
# => FALSE

# silent where the markers are silent: Bela is not confined, Rex is shielded.
? obliged(Review, Bela).
# => FALSE

? obliged(Review, Rex).
# => FALSE

# "Several of the judged are not held" — Rex is the newest of them and was
# missing from the roster above, which is how a count in the prose went stale.
? err(Rex, Isolation).
# => FALSE

# ── the exhibit: what else was available and was not built ───────────────────
# MUST BE LAST. An accepted rule loads into the KB and stays; any query after
# this point is measuring a polluted base. rights-floor.pins.nibli:285 records a
# real vacuous green from forgetting exactly this.
#
# MUST BE LAST is now a choice rather than the only option: nibli 425567b added
# `:accept-scoped`, which retracts the statement afterwards and so cannot
# pollute. It was tried here and refused — the rule is the premise of the query
# below it, and scoped, the accept still passes as loadable while
# `err(Ruk, Duty)` flips to FALSE (measured 2026-08-01). Scoping would keep the
# half of the chapter's claim that the stratifier permits this rule and lose the
# half that says it derives.
#
# "The design could mark the review body for having ignored the duty; that rule
#  was tried and it works." This is that rule. It makes err and obliged mutually
# recursive over positive edges and the stratifier permits it, which is why the
# chapter can no longer call the powerlessness structural.
:accept
all $x: obliged(Review, $x) & ~capture(Review, $x) -> err($x, Duty).

? err(Ruk, Duty).
# => TRUE
